# flux-db Style Rules

Baseline: no rustfmt.toml, clippy.toml, [lints] or CI exist yet. These rules follow default rustfmt, default clippy and the Rust API Guidelines. Update this file when config is added.

## Project Layout and Toolchain
- Workspace crates: `crates/core` (`flux-db-core`), `crates/sql-render` (`flux-db-sql-render`), `crates/postgres` (`flux-db-postgres`), `crates/entity` (`flux-db-entity`), `crates/flux-db` (facade `flux-db`). Architecture rules live in `architecture.mdc`.
- Toolchain: latest stable Rust, edition 2024, workspace `rust-version = "1.99"` (set in the root `Cargo.toml`). Modern syntax and std APIs up to that version are welcome.
- Put shared lint and format settings in the workspace root (`[workspace.lints]`, `rustfmt.toml`); member crates inherit with `[lints] workspace = true`.

## Language
- All code, identifiers, comments, docs, commit-facing text and error messages are in English.

## Formatting
- Code must pass `cargo fmt --check` with default rustfmt settings. Do not hand-format against rustfmt.
- Code must pass `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`. Prefer fixing over `#[allow]`; any `#[allow(...)]` needs a one-line comment explaining why, scoped as narrowly as possible.
- Do not enable `clippy::pedantic` crate-wide unless it is added to config; pedantic suggestions may be applied case by case when they improve clarity.

## Naming (Rust API Guidelines)
- `UpperCamelCase` for types, traits, enum variants; `snake_case` for functions, methods, modules, variables; `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for consts and statics.
- Acronyms are one word: `SqlBackend`, `NoSqlStore`, not `SQLBackend`.
- Conversions: `as_*` (cheap borrow), `to_*` (expensive or owned), `into_*` (consumes self).
- Getters have no `get_` prefix (`fn name(&self)`); iterator methods are `iter`, `iter_mut`, `into_iter`.
- Builder methods take and return `self` and are named after the field they set.
- Booleans read as predicates: `is_*`, `has_*`, `can_*`.

## Module Layout
- One concept per module; keep files focused (aim under ~400 lines).
- Use `foo.rs` + `foo/` directory style, not `mod.rs`.
- Keep items private by default; widen to `pub(crate)` before `pub`. Re-export the public API from `lib.rs` deliberately.
- Order inside a file: `use` statements, constants, types, trait impls, inherent impls, free functions, `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`.
- Group imports: `std`, external crates, then `crate`/`super`; no glob imports except in preludes and tests.

## Error Handling
- Library code never panics on recoverable conditions: no `unwrap`/`expect` outside tests, examples and proven invariants (document the invariant in the `expect` message).
- Return `Result<T, flux_db_core::Error>` (the single `#[non_exhaustive]` enum defined in `architecture.mdc`); do not add new error types or variants without an architecture change. Use `?` for propagation.
- Error messages are lowercase, without trailing punctuation, and describe what failed.
- Do not erase error context: pass driver errors through untouched in `Error::Driver` instead of stringifying them.

## Documentation
- Every public item has a `///` rustdoc comment: a one-line summary, then details if needed.
- Include an `# Examples` section with a compiling doctest for public functions, methods and types where usage is non-obvious.
- Add `# Errors` when a function returns `Result`, listing when each error occurs.
- Add `# Panics` when a function can panic, and `# Safety` on every `unsafe fn` (plus a `// SAFETY:` comment on every `unsafe` block).
- Modules and the crate root have `//!` docs describing their purpose.
- Comments explain *why*, not *what*. Remove commented-out code and stale TODOs (TODOs must reference an issue).

## Clean Code
- No redundant logic: remove dead code, duplicate branches, needless clones, needless `return`, and `match` that could be `if let`/combinators.
- Prefer borrowing (`&str`, `&[T]`, `impl AsRef<..>`) in parameters over owned types when ownership is not needed.
- Prefer iterators and combinators over manual index loops when clearer.
- Keep functions short and single-purpose; extract helpers instead of nesting deeper than ~3 levels.
- Avoid magic numbers; name them as constants.
- Derive common traits (`Debug`, `Clone`, `PartialEq`, `Default` when meaningful) on public types.
- Refactors must not change behaviour; run tests after every style pass.
